\subsection{Net charge and conservation}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
  \item \textbf{Neutral:} net charge $= 0$ (equal $+$ and $-$ overall).
  \item \textbf{Net charge:} excess of one sign.
  \item \textbf{Conservation of charge:} in an isolated system the net charge
        is constant. Charging \emph{separates} $+q$ and $-q$; it does not
        create charge from nothing.
\end{itemize}
SI unit: \textbf{coulomb} (C). Typical rubbed-object charges are $\sim 10^{-9}$~C.
Also: $1\,\mathrm{mC}=10^{-3}\,\mathrm{C}$, $1\,\mu\mathrm{C}=10^{-6}\,\mathrm{C}$.

\subsection{Interaction between charges}
Like charges \textbf{repel}; unlike charges \textbf{attract}.
The two forces form an action--reaction pair (equal magnitude, opposite direction).

