\section{Electric field}

\subsection{Definition}
The electric field is the region where a charge feels an electric force.
Field strength at a point:
\[
\vec E = \frac{\vec F}{q_{+}}
\quad\Rightarrow\quad
\vec F = q\vec E.
\]
Unit: $\mathrm{N\,C^{-1}}$ ($= \mathrm{V\,m^{-1}}$).
Direction of $\vec E$ $=$ direction of force on a \textbf{positive} test charge.

\subsection{Field of a point charge}
\[
E = k\frac{|Q|}{r^{2}},
\qquad
\begin{cases}
+Q: & \text{radially outwards},\\
-Q: & \text{radially inwards}.
\end{cases}
\]
(The source does not exert a force on itself.)

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
  \node[circle,draw=posC,fill=posC!20,minimum size=7mm,font=\bfseries] at (0,0) {$+$};
  \foreach \a in {0,45,...,315} {
