%======================================================================
\section{What is electric charge?}

\subsection{Starting from what you already know}
You have already met electrostatic effects long before any formula:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Rub a balloon on your hair: the balloon sticks to a wall; your hair stands up.
  \item Take off a wool jumper in a dry room and hear tiny crackles; in the dark you
        may even see sparks.
  \item Walk on a carpet and get a shock from a metal door handle.
\end{itemize}
In each case something invisible has changed about the objects involved.
Physics names that ``something'' \textbf{electric charge}.

Charge is not a fluid poured onto an object, and it is not a separate substance
you can scrape off like dirt. It is a \textbf{fundamental property of matter}
--- like mass --- that tells us how a particle responds to electric forces.

\subsection{The atomic origin of charge}
All ordinary matter is made of atoms. An atom has:
\begin{itemize}
  \item a tiny dense \textbf{nucleus} containing \textbf{protons} (charge $+e$)
        and usually neutrons (charge~$0$);
  \item a cloud of \textbf{electrons} (charge $-e$) surrounding the nucleus.
\end{itemize}
The elementary charge is
\[
e = 1.60\times 10^{-19}\,\mathrm{C}.
\]
A proton and an electron have charges equal in size and opposite in sign.
In a normal atom the number of protons equals the number of electrons, so the
atom's \textbf{net charge is zero}: we call it \textbf{neutral}.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  \draw[thick] (0,0) circle (1.45);
  \fill[posC] (0,0) circle (3pt);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,align=center] at (0,-0.45) {nucleus\\($+$ protons)};
  \foreach \a in {30,150,270} {
    \fill[negC] (\a:1.15) circle (2.2pt);
  }
  \node[font=\scriptsize,negC] at (1.55,1.0) {electrons $-$};
  \node[below,font=\small] at (0,-1.75) {Neutral atom: equal $+e$ and $-e$ overall};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Important:} charge is \emph{carried by} protons and electrons. You cannot
have ``charge without particles''. When an everyday object becomes charged, what
has really happened is that the \emph{numbers} of protons and electrons in it are
no longer equal.

\subsection{How everyday objects become charged}
In solids, protons are locked inside nuclei and do not wander about.
\textbf{Only electrons move} from one object to another in ordinary electrostatic
experiments.

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Object A loses electrons} $\Rightarrow$ it has more protons than
        electrons $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{net positive} charge.
  \item \textbf{Object B gains those electrons} $\Rightarrow$ it has more electrons
        than protons $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{net negative} charge.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.95]
  \draw[thick,rounded corners] (-0.2,-0.9) rectangle (1.8,0.9);
  \node[font=\small\bfseries] at (0.8,0.45) {acetate};
  \node[posC,font=\Large\bfseries] at (0.8,-0.25) {$+$};
  \draw[thick,rounded corners] (3.6,-0.9) rectangle (5.6,0.9);
  \node[font=\small\bfseries] at (4.6,0.45) {duster};
  \node[negC,font=\Large\bfseries] at (4.6,-0.25) {$-$};
  \draw[-{Latex},very thick] (1.95,0.15) -- (3.45,0.15)
    node[midway,above,font=\scriptsize] {electrons};
  \node[below,align=center,font=\small] at (2.7,-1.25)
    {Rubbing: electrons leave acetate $\to$ acetate $+$, duster $-$\\
     (net charge of the \emph{pair} is still zero)};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

Different materials hold electrons with different strengths. Rubbing forces some
electrons across the contact surface --- that is charging by \textbf{friction}.
Typical rubbed-object charges are only nanocoulombs ($\sim 10^{-9}$~C), tiny
compared with $1$~C, but still enough for strong local forces because $e$ itself
is so small that $10^{-9}$~C still means $\sim 10^{10}$ electrons.

\subsection{Two kinds of charge --- and why the names are ``$+$'' and ``$-$''}
Experiments show there are exactly \textbf{two} kinds of charge. Benjamin Franklin
chose the labels \textbf{positive} and \textbf{negative}; the choice is a convention,
not a discovery about which is ``better''. What is not conventional is the
interaction rule, confirmed by countless experiments:

\begin{center}
\large
\textbf{Like charges repel; unlike charges attract.}
\end{center}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \begin{scope}
    \node[circle,draw=posC,fill=posC!15,minimum size=8mm,font=\bfseries] (P1) at (0,0) {$+$};
    \node[circle,draw=posC,fill=posC!15,minimum size=8mm,font=\bfseries] (P2) at (2.4,0) {$+$};
    \draw[-{Latex},thick] (P1.west) -- ++(-0.9,0);
    \draw[-{Latex},thick] (P2.east) -- ++(0.9,0);
    \node[below=10pt,font=\small] at (1.2,0) {like $\Rightarrow$ repel};
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[shift={(5.6,0)}]
    \node[circle,draw=posC,fill=posC!15,minimum size=8mm,font=\bfseries] (Q1) at (0,0) {$+$};
    \node[circle,draw=negC,fill=negC!15,minimum size=8mm,font=\bfseries] (Q2) at (2.4,0) {$-$};
    \draw[-{Latex},thick] (Q1.east) -- ++(0.75,0);
    \draw[-{Latex},thick] (Q2.west) -- ++(-0.75,0);
    \node[below=10pt,font=\small] at (1.2,0) {unlike $\Rightarrow$ attract};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

The two forces on a pair of charges are an \textbf{action--reaction pair}: equal
magnitude, opposite direction, along the line joining them.

\subsection{Net charge, units, and quantisation}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Net charge} of an object $=$ (total proton charge) $+$ (total electron charge).
        Neutral $\Leftrightarrow$ net charge $=0$.
  \item SI unit: \textbf{coulomb} (C). Also $\mathrm{mC}$, $\mu\mathrm{C}$, $\mathrm{nC}$.
  \item Roughly, $1\,\mathrm{C}$ is the charge of about $6\times 10^{18}$ electrons
        --- a huge amount for static experiments.
  \item \textbf{Quantisation:} any transferable charge comes in whole numbers of $e$:
        $q = \pm Ne$ with integer $N$. (DSE rarely needs this beyond knowing $e$.)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Conservation of charge}
Charge is never created or destroyed in ordinary processes. When you charge a
balloon by rubbing:
\begin{itemize}
  \item the balloon gains electrons (say $-q$);
  \item your hair / cloth loses the same electrons (net $+q$);
  \item the \textbf{sum} for the isolated pair is still the original net charge
        (usually zero).
\end{itemize}
We state this as the \textbf{conservation of charge}:
\begin{quote}
\emph{In an isolated system, the algebraic sum of charge remains constant.}
\end{quote}
``Charging'' always means \textbf{separating} $+q$ and $-q$, not manufacturing
charge from nothing.

\begin{mdframed}[style=misbox]
\noindent\textbf{Common mistake.}
``A neutral object contains no charged particles.'' --- False.
A neutral object contains enormous numbers of protons and electrons; their charges
\emph{cancel}. Neutrality is about the \emph{net}, not about emptiness.
\end{mdframed}

\worked{2012}{24}{B}{86}{2012_q24.png}{%
Translate the observations into signs.
$P$--$Q$ repel $\Rightarrow$ same sign;
$R$--$S$ repel $\Rightarrow$ same sign;
$Q$--$R$ attract $\Rightarrow$ opposite signs.
Hence $\{P,Q\}$ share one sign and $\{R,S\}$ share the other.
(1)~both $P$ and $R$ negative: impossible (they must be opposite).
(2)~both $Q$ and $S$ positive: impossible (they must be opposite).
(3)~$P$ positive and $S$ negative: possible ($P,Q$ positive; $R,S$ negative).
Only (3) works $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{B}.}

%======================================================================
\section{Conductors, insulators and charging methods}

\subsection{Why some materials ``conduct''}
Every material contains charged particles, but they are not equally free to move.
\begin{itemize}
  \item In a \textbf{metal}, some outer electrons are weakly held. They form a sea of
        \textbf{free electrons} that can drift through the lattice of positive ions.
        Metals are good \textbf{conductors}.
  \item In \textbf{salt water}, the salt dissolves into mobile positive and negative
        \textbf{ions}; both can move. So ionic solutions conduct.
  \item In an \textbf{insulator} (plastic, nylon, glass, dry air, pure water),
        electrons are tightly bound. Charge placed on an insulator tends to stay put.
  \item A \textbf{vacuum} contains no charge carriers, so it is an insulator --- even
        though a free charge placed in vacuum can of course move through empty space
        under a force.
\end{itemize}
Your body and the Earth are fairly good conductors (moisture / ions). That is why
touching a charged object can give you a shock: charge suddenly flows through you
to Earth.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.92]
  \draw[thick] (0,0) circle (1.15);
  \foreach \p in {(-0.5,0.35),(0.15,0.55),(-0.25,-0.45),(0.45,-0.25),(0.05,0.05),(0.55,0.2)} {
    \fill[negC] \p circle (1.7pt);
  }
  \node[font=\scriptsize,align=center] at (0,-1.55) {(a) Metal:\\free $e^{-}$ roam};
  \begin{scope}[shift={(4.4,0)}]
    \draw[thick] (0,0) circle (1.15);
    \foreach \a in {0,60,...,300} {
      \fill[gray!65] (\a:0.5) circle (1.5pt);
      \fill[negC!80] (\a:0.5)++(10:0.2) circle (1.2pt);
    }
    \node[font=\scriptsize,align=center] at (0,-1.55) {(b) Insulator:\\charges fixed\\(can still polarise)};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\subsection{Charging methods (Active Physics)}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Friction:} electron transfer by rubbing (see above).
  \item \textbf{Sharing / contact:} two conductors touch; free charge redistributes
        over the combined conductor, then they separate carrying shares of the charge.
  \item \textbf{Induction:} a charged object is brought \emph{near} a conductor
        without touching. Free charges in the conductor are pushed or pulled, so
        charge \textbf{separates} on the conductor. If you briefly earth the
        conductor and then remove the inducer, the conductor can be left with a
        net charge \textbf{opposite} to the inducing charge.
  \item \textbf{EHT (extra-high tension) supply:} a high-voltage source delivers
        charge deliberately (school Van de Graaff / EHT pack).
\end{enumerate}
\textbf{Earthing} means connecting to Earth so that charge can flow to or from a
vast reservoir until the object's potential matches Earth's (often taken as $V=0$).

\subsection{Why a charged object attracts a neutral one}
A charged rod attracts small scraps of paper or a neutral metal leaf. Why, if the
paper is ``neutral''?

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Neutral conductor:} the rod's field redistributes free electrons.
        The near side becomes opposite in sign to the rod, the far side the same
        sign. The near-side attraction is stronger than the far-side repulsion
        (because force falls with distance), so the net force is attractive.
  \item \textbf{Neutral insulator:} molecules become \textbf{polarised} (centres of
        $+$ and $-$ shift slightly) and line up. Again the near side is opposite
        to the rod $\Rightarrow$ net attraction.
\end{itemize}
A charged object does \textbf{not} attract a single neutral elementary particle
with no internal structure to polarise --- but every real macroscopic ``neutral
object'' can redistribute or polarise.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \node[circle,draw=posC,fill=posC!20,minimum size=10mm,font=\bfseries] (R) at (0,0) {$+$};
  \draw[thick] (2.1,-0.75) rectangle (3.9,0.75);
  \foreach \y in {-0.4,0,0.4} {
    \node[font=\scriptsize,negC] at (2.4,\y) {$-$};
    \node[font=\scriptsize,posC] at (3.6,\y) {$+$};
  }
  \draw[-{Latex},thick] (1.95,0) -- (0.6,0);
  \node[below,font=\small,align=center] at (2.0,-1.15)
    {Induction: near side opposite $\Rightarrow$ net attraction};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{mdframed}[style=tipbox]
\noindent\textbf{Suspended conducting spheres --- exam checklist.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Same net sign $\Rightarrow$ always repel.
  \item Opposite net signs $\Rightarrow$ always attract.
  \item Charged + uncharged conductor $\Rightarrow$ attract (induction).
  \item Two uncharged conductors $\Rightarrow$ no electrostatic attraction.
  \item If \emph{every} pair among three spheres attracts: typically one is
        uncharged and the other two are unlike.
\end{itemize}
\end{mdframed}

\worked{2015}{21}{B}{73}{2015_q21.png}{%
``Any two attract.'' If two balls had the same nonzero sign they would repel ---
forbidden. All three charged with a pattern that makes every pair attract is
impossible for the same reason. The consistent picture is: \textbf{one uncharged},
other two \textbf{unlike}. Unlike pair attracts; each charged ball attracts the
neutral one by induction. Option~A fails because like charges would repel.
$\Rightarrow$ \textbf{B}.}

%======================================================================
\section{Coulomb's law}

Qualitative rules (attract / repel) are not enough for calculation. Coulomb's law
gives the \textbf{magnitude} of the force between two point charges in vacuum
(air is close enough for DSE):
\[
F = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{|Qq|}{r^{2}}
  = k\frac{|Qq|}{r^{2}},
\qquad
k \approx 9.0\times 10^{9}\,\mathrm{N\,m^{2}\,C^{-2}}.
\]
Here $\varepsilon_0$ is the permittivity of free space.
\begin{itemize}
  \item Direction: along the joining line; sense from the like/unlike rule.
  \item Inverse-square: double $r$ $\Rightarrow$ force becomes $\tfrac14$.
  \item Action--reaction: each charge feels force of the same size.
  \item Scaling: charges $\times n$, separation $\times m$ $\Rightarrow$
        $F'=(n^{2}/m^{2})F$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \node[circle,draw,fill=gray!12,minimum size=9mm,font=\small] (A) at (0,0) {$Q$};
  \node[circle,draw,fill=gray!12,minimum size=9mm,font=\small] (B) at (4.4,0) {$q$};
  \draw[{Latex}-{Latex},thick] (A) -- (B) node[midway,above] {$r$};
  \draw[-{Latex},thick,posC] ($(A)+(0.6,0.5)$) -- ++(1.1,0) node[above,font=\scriptsize] {$F$};
  \draw[-{Latex},thick,posC] ($(B)+(-0.6,0.5)$) -- ++(-1.1,0) node[above,font=\scriptsize] {$F$};
  \node[below,font=\small] at (2.2,-0.75) {Equal-and-opposite Coulomb forces};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{mdframed}[style=tipbox]
\noindent\textbf{Problem-solving habit (Active Physics).}
(1)~Decide attract or repel for each pair.
(2)~Draw a force diagram on the charge of interest.
(3)~Compute each magnitude with Coulomb's law.
(4)~Resolve components and add.
\end{mdframed}

\worked{2026}{21}{B}{}{2026_q21.png}{%
Originally $F=k|q_1 q_2|/r^{2}$.
After doubling both charges and doubling the separation:
$F'=k\dfrac{(2q_1)(2q_2)}{(2r)^{2}}=k\dfrac{4q_1 q_2}{4r^{2}}=F$.
The force is unchanged $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{B}.}

%======================================================================
\section{Electric field}

\subsection{Why introduce a field?}
Coulomb's law needs both charges. Often we want to describe the influence of a
set of fixed source charges on any test charge we might place later.
We invent the \textbf{electric field}: a property of space set up by the sources.
A charge $q$ placed in the field then feels
\[
\vec F = q\vec E.
\]

\subsection{Definition of electric field strength}
At any point,
\[
\vec E = \frac{\vec F}{q_{+}}
\]
where $q_{+}$ is a small \textbf{positive} test charge. So:
\begin{itemize}
  \item unit of $E$: $\mathrm{N\,C^{-1}}$ (same as $\mathrm{V\,m^{-1}}$);
  \item direction of $\vec E$ $=$ direction of force on a positive charge;
  \item a negative charge feels a force \textbf{opposite} to $\vec E$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Field of a single point charge}
\[
E = k\frac{|Q|}{r^{2}},
\qquad
\begin{cases}
+Q: & \vec E\text{ radially outwards},\\
-Q: & \vec E\text{ radially inwards}.
\end{cases}
\]
(The source does not act on itself.)

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.88]
  \node[circle,draw=posC,fill=posC!20,minimum size=7mm,font=\bfseries] at (0,0) {$+$};
  \foreach \a in {0,45,...,315} {\draw[-{Latex},thick,gray!65] (\a:0.55) -- (\a:1.6);}
  \node[below,font=\small] at (0,-1.9) {$+Q$: outwards};
  \begin{scope}[shift={(4.6,0)}]
    \node[circle,draw=negC,fill=negC!20,minimum size=7mm,font=\bfseries] at (0,0) {$-$};
    \foreach \a in {0,45,...,315} {\draw[-{Latex},thick,gray!65] (\a:1.6) -- (\a:0.55);}
    \node[below,font=\small] at (0,-1.9) {$-Q$: inwards};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\subsection{Electric field lines}
Field lines are a drawing language for $\vec E$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item the \textbf{tangent} gives the direction of $\vec E$;
  \item the \textbf{density} of lines indicates the magnitude of $E$;
  \item lines start on $+$ (or infinity) and end on $-$ (or infinity);
  \item lines never cross (that would mean two directions for $\vec E$ at once).
\end{itemize}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.05cm,y=0.52cm]
  \foreach \y in {-2,-1,0,1,2} {\draw[-{Latex},thick] (0,\y) -- (2.1,\y);}
  \foreach \y in {-2,-1,0,1,2} {\draw[-{Latex},thick] (2.9,\y*0.7) -- (4.1,\y*0.32);}
  \foreach \y in {-2,-1,0,1,2} {\draw[-{Latex},thick] (4.9,\y*0.32) -- (6.7,\y*0.95);}
  \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.05,-2.55) {$P$ medium};
  \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3.5,-2.55) {$Q$ strongest};
  \node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (5.8,-2.55) {$R$ weakest};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\worked{2023}{24}{C}{62}{2023_q24.png}{%
Read line density: $Q$ densest (largest $E$), $R$ sparsest (smallest $E$),
$P$ in between. Ascending order $E_R < E_P < E_Q$ $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{C}.}

\subsection{Superposition --- several sources}
If several source charges are present, each produces its own field, and
\[
\vec E_{\mathrm{net}} = \vec E_1 + \vec E_2 + \cdots
\]
(vector sum). On a straight line this is signed addition; off the line, resolve
into components.

\subsection{Where can the field be zero? (neutral point)}
A \textbf{neutral point} is where $\vec E_{\mathrm{net}}=\vec 0$.
For two point charges on a line:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.2cm}p{9.5cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Charges} & \textbf{Where $E$ can vanish} \\
\midrule
Same sign & Outside the pair, nearer the \emph{smaller} $|Q|$
  (fields oppose only outside). \\
Opposite signs & Outside, on the side of the \emph{smaller} $|Q|$.
  \emph{Never between them}: between opposite charges both fields point the
  same way, so they reinforce. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Magnitudes equal when
$k|Q_1|/r_1^{2} = k|Q_2|/r_2^{2}$, i.e.\ $r_1/r_2=\sqrt{|Q_1|/|Q_2|}$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.82cm]
  \node[circle,draw,fill=posC!18,font=\scriptsize\bfseries,minimum size=6.5mm] at (0,0) {$+2Q$};
  \node[circle,draw,fill=negC!18,font=\scriptsize\bfseries,minimum size=6.5mm] at (5,0) {$-Q$};
  \draw[dashed] (-1.4,0) -- (7.3,0);
  \fill (2.5,0) circle (1.4pt) node[below=2pt,font=\scriptsize] {$M$};
  \fill (6.35,0) circle (1.4pt) node[above=2pt,font=\scriptsize] {$Z$};
  \node[below,font=\small,align=center] at (2.8,-0.85)
    {Opposite charges: look for $E=0$ outside, beside the smaller charge};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\worked{2015}{22}{D}{33}{2015_q22.png}{%
$-4Q$ and $+Q$ are opposite, so $E=0$ cannot lie between them.
It must be outside, on the side of the smaller charge $+Q$ (to the right).
Equal magnitudes need distance to $-4Q$ twice the distance to $+Q$.
With 3~grid units between the charges, that point is 3~units right of $+Q$
$\Rightarrow$ \textbf{Z} $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{D}.}

\worked{2012}{25}{B}{49}{2012_q25.png}{%
(1)~Field is a vector: for opposite charges, $E=0$ only outside beside smaller
$-Q$ $\Rightarrow$ point \textbf{Z}.
(2)~Potential is a scalar: $k(2Q)/r_{+}+k(-Q)/r_{-}=0\Rightarrow r_{+}=2r_{-}$.
Between the charges that is closer to $-Q$ $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{Y}.
$\Rightarrow$ \textbf{B}.
(This question is the classic reminder that $E=0$ and $V=0$ are different conditions.)}

\subsection{Combining fields in 2D}
Same method as combining forces: draw each $\vec E$, use symmetry, add components.
Squares and equilateral triangles appear often because distances are equal or
related by $\sqrt{2}$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.95]
  \draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (2.5,2.5);
  \node[circle,draw=posC,fill=posC!18,minimum size=6mm,font=\scriptsize\bfseries] at (0,2.5) {$+$};
  \node[circle,draw=negC,fill=negC!18,minimum size=6mm,font=\scriptsize\bfseries] at (2.5,0) {$-$};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (-0.25,-0.2) {$O$};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (-0.25,2.7) {$P$};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (2.75,-0.2) {$N$};
  \draw[-{Latex},thick,blue!55!black] (0,0.2) -- (0,1.15);
  \draw[-{Latex},thick,blue!55!black] (0.2,0) -- (1.15,0);
  \draw[-{Latex},very thick,red!65!black] (0,0) -- (0.95,0.95);
  \node[right,font=\scriptsize] at (1.05,1.1) {$\vec E_{\mathrm{net}}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\worked{2025}{24}{A}{66}{2025_q24.png}{%
At corner $O$: field due to $+Q$ at $P$ has size $kQ/r^{2}$ and points away from $P$
(downward); field due to $-Q$ at $N$ has size $kQ/r^{2}$ and points toward $N$
(rightward). Perpendicular equal vectors give magnitude
$\sqrt{2}\,kQ/r^{2}=\dfrac{\sqrt{2}\,Q}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 r^{2}}$,
direction along the diagonal from $P$ toward $N$ $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{A}.}

\worked{2019}{23}{A}{}{2019_q23.png}{%
$X$ and $Z$ are identical positives; $Z$ is fixed. For $X$ to be in equilibrium,
the middle charge $Y$ must be \textbf{negative}: it attracts $X$ rightward,
balancing $Z$'s leftward repulsion.
If $Y$ is pushed toward $Z$, it moves farther from $X$, so the attractive pull on
$X$ weakens while $Z$'s repulsion is unchanged $\Rightarrow$ net force on $X$ is
to the left $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{A}.}

%======================================================================
\section{Uniform electric field}

\subsection{Parallel plates}
A field is \textbf{uniform} if $\vec E$ is the same (magnitude and direction) at
every point in a region. Between two large parallel metal plates held at potential
difference $V$ and separated by $d$, the field in the middle is nearly uniform:
\[
E = \frac{V}{d},
\]
directed from the higher-potential plate toward the lower-potential plate
(from the $+$ plate toward the $-$ plate if those are the polarities).

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \draw[very thick] (0,0) -- (0,2.7) node[above,font=\small] {$+$};
  \draw[very thick] (3.3,0) -- (3.3,2.7) node[above,font=\small] {$-$};
  \foreach \y in {0.35,0.85,1.35,1.85,2.35} {
    \draw[-{Latex},thick,gray!65] (0.25,\y) -- (3.05,\y);
  }
  \draw[{Latex}-{Latex}] (0,-0.4) -- (3.3,-0.4) node[midway,below,font=\scriptsize] {$d$};
  \node[font=\small] at (1.65,3.15) {$E=V/d$\,; lines straight, parallel, evenly spaced};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\worked{2014}{22}{D}{61}{2014_q22.png}{%
Magnitude: $E=F/|e|=(8.0\times 10^{-18})/(1.6\times 10^{-19})=50\,\mathrm{N\,C^{-1}}$.
The force on the electron is toward $P$. Because the electron is negative, $\vec E$
is opposite to that force, i.e.\ from $P$ toward $Q$ $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{D}.}

\subsection{Floating charged droplet (Millikan idea)}
A droplet of mass $m$ and charge $q$ floats when electric force balances weight:
\[
qE = mg \quad\Rightarrow\quad E=\frac{mg}{q},\qquad q=\frac{mgd}{V}.
\]
Polarity matters: for a \textbf{positive} drop, $\vec E$ must point \textbf{upward}
so that $q\vec E$ is upward.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \draw[very thick] (-1.7,1.7) -- (1.7,1.7);
  \draw[very thick] (-1.7,0) -- (1.7,0);
  \filldraw (0,0.85) circle (2.6pt);
  \draw[-{Latex},thick] (0,0.85) -- (0,1.4) node[right,font=\scriptsize] {$qE$};
  \draw[-{Latex},thick] (0,0.85) -- (0,0.3) node[right,font=\scriptsize] {$mg$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\worked{2018}{22}{A}{55}{2018_q22.png}{%
Floating $+Q$: need upward electric force to cancel $mg$.
Force on a positive charge is parallel to $\vec E$, so $\vec E$ is upward and
$E=mg/Q$ $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{A}.}

\subsection{Motion of a charged particle in a uniform field}
Unless the question is about floating, neglect gravity.
\begin{itemize}
  \item Acceleration $\vec a = q\vec E/m$ is constant in a uniform field.
  \item If the initial velocity is perpendicular to $\vec E$, the path is a
        \textbf{parabola} --- same mathematics as projectile motion under gravity.
  \item Speed changes when the particle moves through a potential difference:
        $\Delta\big(\tfrac12 mv^{2}\big)=q\,\Delta V$ (careful with signs; often use
        magnitudes with a clear energy argument).
\end{itemize}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \draw[very thick] (0,0) -- (4.3,0);
  \draw[very thick] (0,2.1) -- (4.3,2.1);
  \foreach \x in {0.45,1.25,2.05,2.85,3.65} {
    \draw[-{Latex},gray!55] (\x,1.95) -- (\x,0.15);
  }
  \draw[-{Latex},thick] (-0.15,1.05) -- (0.45,1.05) node[above,font=\scriptsize] {$v$};
  \draw[thick,domain=0:3.3,smooth,variable=\x,blue!60!black]
    plot ({\x},{1.05-0.11*\x*\x});
  \node[font=\scriptsize,align=center] at (2.15,-0.55)
    {Negative charge: force opposite $\vec E$ $\Rightarrow$ curves against the arrows};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\worked{2025}{23}{D}{63}{2025_q23.png}{%
Particle $X$ is negative, so its force is opposite to the drawn field lines.
With $\vec v$ perpendicular to $\vec E$, the trajectory is a parabola bending in
the force direction --- matching option \textbf{D}.}

%======================================================================
\section{Electric potential and energy}

\subsection{From force to energy}
Force tells you how hard a charge is pushed. Often we care about \textbf{work} and
\textbf{energy}: how much work is needed to move a charge from one place to another.
That leads to electric potential.

\subsection{Definition of potential}
The electric potential $V$ at a point is defined as the work done by an external
agent per unit positive charge in bringing a test charge slowly (no KE change)
from infinity to that point, taking $V(\infty)=0$:
\[
V = \frac{U}{q},\qquad [V]=\mathrm{V}=\mathrm{J\,C^{-1}}.
\]
Equivalently, a positive charge tends to move spontaneously from high $V$ to low $V$.

\subsection{Potential of a point charge}
\[
V = k\frac{Q}{r}
\quad\text{(signed: positive near $+Q$, negative near $-Q$).}
\]
Potential is a \textbf{scalar}: potentials from several sources simply add
\[
V_{\mathrm{net}} = V_1 + V_2 + \cdots.
\]

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.1cm,y=0.85cm]
  \draw[-{Latex},thick] (0,0) -- (5.3,0) node[right,font=\scriptsize] {$r$};
  \draw[-{Latex},thick] (0,-1.7) -- (0,2.5) node[above,font=\scriptsize] {$V$};
  \draw[thick,domain=0.55:5.1,smooth,variable=\x,posC] plot ({\x},{1.9/\x});
  \draw[thick,domain=0.55:5.1,smooth,variable=\x,negC] plot ({\x},{-1.5/\x});
  \node[posC,font=\scriptsize] at (3.9,0.95) {$+Q$};
  \node[negC,font=\scriptsize] at (3.9,-0.95) {$-Q$};
  \node[below,font=\small] at (2.6,-2.0) {$V=kQ/r$ falls in magnitude as $1/r$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\subsection{Potential difference and uniform fields}
The potential difference between two points is related to the field by
\[
\Delta V = V_A - V_B = -\int_A^B \vec E\cdot\mathrm{d}\vec\ell.
\]
In a uniform field of strength $E$, over a displacement $d$ parallel to the field,
\[
|\Delta V| = Ed.
\]
That is why $E=V/d$ between parallel plates --- it is the same relation rearranged.

In a uniform field, \textbf{equipotential surfaces} are planes perpendicular to
$\vec E$. Potential changes at a constant rate along the field direction and does
not change when you move perpendicular to $\vec E$.

\begin{mdframed}[style=misbox]
\noindent\textbf{Common mistake --- mixing $E$ and $V$.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item $E=0$ does \emph{not} imply $V=0$, and $V=0$ does not imply $E=0$.
  \item Zero field needs \textbf{vector} cancellation; zero potential needs
        \textbf{signed scalar} cancellation.
  \item Example: midway between $+Q$ and $-Q$, $V$ can be zero while $E$ is large.
\end{itemize}
\end{mdframed}

\worked{2014}{23}{B}{53}{2014_q23.png}{%
For an isolated $+Q$, $V=kQ/r$. Point $X$ is 3~grid units away with $V_X=V$;
point $Y$ is 2~grid units away. Since $V\propto 1/r$,
$V_Y=V\cdot(r_X/r_Y)=V\cdot\tfrac{3}{2}=\tfrac{3}{2}V$ $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{B}.}

\worked{ppp}{25}{---}{}{ppp_q25.png}{%
$V_X=V_Y=5\,\mathrm{V}$, so diagonal $XY$ is an equipotential.
In a uniform field the centre $M$ of square $WXZY$ lies on that equipotential,
so $V_M=5\,\mathrm{V}$. Along diagonal $WZ$, $V$ varies linearly with distance,
hence $V_M=(V_W+V_Z)/2\Rightarrow 5=(1+V_Z)/2\Rightarrow V_Z=9\,\mathrm{V}$
(option~\textbf{C}).}

\subsection{Work and electric potential energy}
Work by an external agent to bring charge $q$ from infinity to a point at potential
$V$ (slowly) is
\[
W_{\mathrm{ext}} = qV.
\]
If several fixed charges create a net potential $V$ at the destination, use that
net value. The change in electrical PE when charge $q$ moves through potential
difference $\Delta V$ is $\Delta U = q\,\Delta V$.

A useful estimate: if charge $Q$ is released through a p.d.\ $\Delta V=Ed$
(approximately uniform field over distance $d$), the energy involved is about
\[
W = Q\,\Delta V = QEd.
\]

\worked{2015}{23}{A}{32}{2015_q23.png}{%
At the empty vertex of the equilateral triangle, distances to $+Q$ and $-Q$ are
both $L$, so
$V=k(+Q)/L+k(-Q)/L=0$.
Energy required to bring another $+Q$ from infinity:
$W=(+Q)V=0$ $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{A}.
(Minimum energy is zero --- the potential energy does not change.)}

\worked{2013}{25}{C}{58}{2013_q25.png}{%
Treat the cloud--ground field as uniform.
$\Delta V=Ed=(3\times 10^{6})(500)=1.5\times 10^{9}\,\mathrm{V}$.
Energy $W=Q\Delta V=20\times 1.5\times 10^{9}=3\times 10^{10}\,\mathrm{J}$,
order of magnitude $10^{10}\,\mathrm{J}$ $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{C}.}

%======================================================================
\section{Formula summary}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
Idea & Formula / rule \\
\midrule
Elementary charge & $e=1.60\times 10^{-19}\,\mathrm{C}$ \\
Coulomb force & $F=k|Qq|/r^{2}$,\quad $k=9.0\times 10^{9}\,\mathrm{N\,m^{2}\,C^{-2}}$ \\
Field definition & $\vec E=\vec F/q$,\quad $\vec F=q\vec E$ \\
Point-charge field & $E=k|Q|/r^{2}$ (out from $+$, in to $-$) \\
Uniform plates & $E=V/d$ \\
Point-charge potential & $V=kQ/r$ (signed, scalar) \\
Uniform-field p.d. & $|\Delta V|=Ed$ \\
Floating drop & $qE=mg$ \\
Work / energy & $W=qV$,\quad $\Delta U=q\,\Delta V$,\quad $W=QEd$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{mdframed}[style=tipbox]
\noindent\textbf{Before the exam --- five checks.}
(1)~Are you talking about force, field, or potential?
(2)~Vector or scalar?
(3)~Sign of the test charge?
(4)~Between opposite charges, can $E$ really be zero there?
(5)~Did conservation of charge / induction explain an ``attraction'' that looks mysterious?
\end{mdframed}

