\section{Short circuit versus overload}

\subsection{Short circuit and overload}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Short circuit:} a very low-resistance accidental path between points
        at different potential (typically L--N or L--E), bypassing the appliance.
        Current becomes huge almost instantly $\Rightarrow$ fuse/MCB must break.
  \item \textbf{Overload:} too many appliances (or one oversized load) drawing a
        current larger than the cable/fuse rating for a sustained time, without a
        near-zero resistance fault. Heating builds up gradually.
\end{itemize}
Both can blow a fuse; a short circuit is more violent and immediate.

\subsection{Which shorts blow the fuse?}

input.new_string:
\section{Why mains electricity is AC}

\subsection{Alternating current at the wall socket}
Every wall socket in Hong Kong delivers \textbf{alternating current} (a.c.):
the voltage between the live and neutral terminals reverses direction
\textbf{50 times every second}. A battery, by contrast, drives
\textbf{direct current} (d.c.): charge flows one way only.

\subsection{Why the grid uses a.c.}
Power stations and the long cables that feed a city must transfer huge power
with as little wasted heating as possible. From circuit theory you already know
$P=VI$ and that cable heating is $I^{2}R$.

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{High voltage, low current.}
        For a fixed power $P$, a larger voltage means a smaller current
        ($I=P/V$). Smaller $I$ means much less $I^{2}R$ heating in the
        transmission cables, so less energy is wasted on the journey.
  \item \textbf{A safe voltage at home.}
        The cables may run at a very high voltage, but what arrives at a
        Hong Kong socket is a lower, standardised value ($220\,\mathrm{V}$
        r.m.s.). Appliances and wiring in the home are designed for that value.
  \item \textbf{Power stations produce a.c.}
        Large generators at power stations naturally produce an alternating
        voltage. The mains supply is therefore a.c.\ from the start.
\end{enumerate}
This chapter is about what happens \emph{after} the electricity reaches the
building: the wires in the wall, plugs, fuses, and how energy use is billed.

input.old_string:
\section{Why mains electricity is AC}

\subsection{Alternating current at the wall socket}
Every wall socket in Hong Kong delivers \textbf{alternating current} (a.c.):
the voltage between the live and neutral terminals reverses direction
\textbf{50 times every second}. Batteries and most phone chargers (after conversion)
use \textbf{direct current} (d.c.). Why did the power system choose a.c.?

\subsection{Why the grid uses a.c.}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Transformers need changing flux.}
        A transformer works by Faraday's law: a changing current in one coil
        induces emf in another. Steady d.c.\ produces no changing flux, so
        you cannot step voltage up or down with a simple transformer.
  \item \textbf{High voltage $\Rightarrow$ low current for the same power.}
        Power delivered is $P=VI$. For fixed $P$, raising $V$ lowers $I$.
        Cable heating loss is $I^{2}R$. Lower $I$ means far less wasted heat
        over long transmission lines.
  \item \textbf{Generation is naturally a.c.}
        Rotating generators (turbines + magnets/coils) produce an alternating emf
        by design. Converting everything to d.c.\ for transmission, then back again,
        is historically more expensive.
\end{enumerate}
So: generate a.c.\ $\to$ step up for transmission $\to$ step down near homes $\to$
deliver a domestic voltage that can be used safely. This chapter focuses on what arrives
at the sockets and how the wiring protects the user.

