\section{Current}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
Quantity & \\
\midrule
Definition & $I=Q/t$ \quad (rate of flow of charge) \\
Unit & ampere (A);\quad $1\,\mathrm{A}=1\,\mathrm{C\,s^{-1}}$ \\
In metals & mobile carriers are electrons; they drift $-$ to $+$ \\
On diagrams & use conventional current ($+$ to $-$) unless asked about electrons \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\note{Current is not ``used up'' by a bulb. In a series loop the same $I$ leaves and returns to the
battery. The bulb converts electrical \textbf{energy}, not charge.}

