\subsection{Skill: equipotential points}

\ex
Ideal wires are equipotential. In the figure, an ideal short joins $X$ to $Y$.
\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.05, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l=$12\,\mathrm{V}$] (0,2.4)
        to[R, l=$4\,\Omega$] (2.5,2.4)
        to[R, l=$4\,\Omega$] (5.0,2.4)
        -- (5.0,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw (2.5,2.4) node[font=\scriptsize,above] {$X$}
        to[R, l_=$8\,\Omega$] (2.5,0)
        node[font=\scriptsize,below] {$Y$};
  \draw[thick,red!70!black] (2.5,2.4) -- (3.3,2.4) -- (3.3,0) -- (2.5,0);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,red!70!black] at (3.85,1.2) {short};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}
Find the current from the cell after the short is connected.
\sol[3.8cm]{%
Short joins $X$ to the bottom rail $\Rightarrow$ $V=0$ across the $8\,\Omega$ and across the right-hand
$4\,\Omega$ (both are bypassed). Only the left $4\,\Omega$ remains across the cell.
$I=12/4=3.0\,\mathrm{A}$.
}

\subsection{Skill: what a short does to a voltage divider}

\ex
\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.05, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l=$12\,\mathrm{V}$] (0,3.0)
        to[R, l=$3\,\Omega$] (0,1.5)
        to[R, l_=$6\,\Omega$] (0,0);
  \draw (0,1.5) -- (1.6,1.5) to[R, l=$6\,\Omega$] (1.6,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw[thick,red!70!black] (0,1.5) -- (-0.7,1.5) -- (-0.7,0) -- (0,0);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,red!70!black] at (-1.15,0.75) {short};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}
With the short connected as shown, find the current from the cell.
\sol[3.8cm]{%
Short joins the mid-node to the bottom rail $\Rightarrow$ both $6\,\Omega$ resistors are shorted out
($V=0$ across each). Only the $3\,\Omega$ remains.
$I=12/3=4.0\,\mathrm{A}$.
}

\subsection{Skill: combine, then split}

\ex
Two $4\,\Omega$ resistors in parallel are placed in series with a $2\,\Omega$ resistor across a $9\,\mathrm{V}$
ideal cell. Find (a)~$R_{\mathrm{eq}}$, (b)~the cell current, (c)~the current in one $4\,\Omega$ branch,
(d)~the power in the $2\,\Omega$ resistor.
\sol[4.5cm]{%
(a) $R_{\parallel}=4/2=2\,\Omega$, $R_{\mathrm{eq}}=2+2=4\,\Omega$.
(b) $I=9/4=2.25\,\mathrm{A}$.
(c) Equal parallel pair: each branch $I/2=1.125\,\mathrm{A}$.
(d) $P=I^{2}R=(2.25)^{2}(2)=10.1\,\mathrm{W}$.
}

