\subsection{What does ``potential'' mean?}

\subsubsection*{Electric potential}

Electric potential at a point is the \textbf{electrical energy per coulomb} at that point,
measured relative to an agreed zero (usually the negative terminal of the cell, or earth).

\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.1, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l={$\mathcal{E}=6\,\mathrm{V}$}] (0,2.8)
        to[R, l=$R$] (3.5,2.8)
        -- (3.5,0) -- (0,0);
  \node[circ,fill=red!75!black,inner sep=1.5pt] at (0,2.8) {};
  \node[circ,fill=blue!70!black,inner sep=1.5pt] at (0,0) {};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,red!75!black,above left] at (0,2.8) {$A$: $V_A=6\,\mathrm{V}$};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,blue!70!black,below left] at (0,0) {$B$: $V_B=0$ (chosen zero)};
\end{circuitikz}
\quad
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline=(current bounding box.center)]
  \node[align=left,font=\small] {
    Unit: volt (V)\\[0.2em]
    $1\,\mathrm{V}=1\,\mathrm{J\,C^{-1}}$\\[0.45em]
    At $A$: each coulomb has\\
    $6\,\mathrm{J}$ of electrical energy\\
    (relative to $B$).
  };
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

Potential is a property of a \emph{point}. Saying ``the potential is $6\,\mathrm{V}$'' always means
``relative to the chosen zero''.

\subsubsection*{Potential difference (p.d.)}

Potential difference between two points is how much the potential \emph{changes} from one to the other.
It equals the energy converted per coulomb moving between those points:
\[
V = \frac{E}{Q}.
\]

\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.1, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l=$6\,\mathrm{V}$] (0,2.6)
        -- (1.2,2.6)
        to[lamp, l=bulb] (3.4,2.6)
        -- (3.4,0) -- (0,0);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,above] at (1.2,2.6) {$P$};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,above] at (3.4,2.6) {$Q$};
  \draw[{Latex}-{Latex}] (1.2,2.15) -- (3.4,2.15)
    node[midway,below,font=\scriptsize] {p.d.\ $V_{PQ}=6\,\mathrm{V}$};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}

\noindent\textit{Example.}
If $2\,\mathrm{C}$ pass through the bulb and $12\,\mathrm{J}$ of electrical energy is converted to light/heat,
then $V=E/Q=12/2=6\,\mathrm{V}$.

A voltmeter reads a \textbf{p.d.}: it compares the potential at its two probes.

\subsubsection*{Electromotive force (e.m.f.)}

e.m.f.\ $\mathcal{E}$ is the energy \emph{gained} by each coulomb \textbf{inside a source}
(chemical energy $\to$ electrical energy in a cell).
It is \emph{not} the energy spent in a bulb; that is a p.d.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.46\textwidth}p{0.46\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{e.m.f.\ $\mathcal{E}$} & \textbf{p.d.\ $V$} \\
\midrule
Where energy is \emph{gained} (inside the cell)
&
Where energy is \emph{converted / spent} (in a load)
\\[0.35em]
$\mathcal{E}=4\,\mathrm{V}$ $\Leftrightarrow$ each coulomb
gains $4\,\mathrm{J}$ in the battery
&
$V=1\,\mathrm{V}$ across a bulb $\Leftrightarrow$ each coulomb
loses $1\,\mathrm{J}$ in that bulb
\\[0.35em]
Symbol: $\mathcal{E}$ (script E)
&
Symbol: $V$ (also called voltage)
\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.05, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l={$\mathcal{E}=4\,\mathrm{V}$\\gain}] (0,2.5)
        to[R, l={$R_1$}, a={spend $V_1$}] (3.0,2.5)
        to[R, l_={$R_2$}, a^={spend $V_2$}] (3.0,0)
        -- (0,0);
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}

\noindent\textit{Example.}
A cell of e.m.f.\ $1.5\,\mathrm{V}$ gives $1.5\,\mathrm{J}$ of electrical energy to every coulomb that
passes through it. If $4\,\mathrm{C}$ pass through the cell, energy supplied $=1.5\times 4=6\,\mathrm{J}$.

\subsubsection*{Equipotential}

Two points are equipotential if they are at the \textbf{same} potential.
Then the p.d.\ between them is zero: no energy is converted when charge moves from one to the other
along an ideal path joining them.

\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.05, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1] (0,2.4)
        -- (4.0,2.4) -- (4.0,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw (1.0,2.4) to[R, l=$R$] (3.0,2.4);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (0.4,2.65) {$A$};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (3.6,2.65) {$C$};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (2.0,0.25) {$B$};
  \draw[thick,red!75!black] (0,2.4) -- (1.0,2.4);
  \draw[thick,red!75!black] (0,2.4) -- (0,1.6);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,red!75!black] at (-0.7,2.0) {same\\potential};
  \draw[thick,blue!70!black] (3.0,2.4) -- (4.0,2.4) -- (4.0,0) -- (0,0);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,blue!70!black] at (4.6,1.2) {same\\potential};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}

\noindent\textit{Example.}
Ideal connecting wires have (almost) zero resistance, so every point on one continuous wire is
equipotential. That is why we paint a whole rail one colour: p.d.\ along that wire is $0$.
A short circuit forces two points to become equipotential.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.22\textwidth}p{0.72\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Term & In one line \\
\midrule
Potential & Energy per coulomb \emph{at a point} (needs a zero). \\
p.d. & Energy converted per coulomb \emph{between two points}: $V=E/Q$. \\
e.m.f. & Energy gained per coulomb \emph{inside a source}. \\
Equipotential & Same potential $\Rightarrow$ p.d.\ $=0$ between those points. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

