\subsection{What does ``potential'' mean?}

\subsubsection*{Water-pipe analogy}

A closed water circuit is a useful picture of a simple electric circuit:

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.95]
  % left: water
  \draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (0.7,1.8);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,align=center] at (0.35,2.15) {pump};
  \draw[thick,blue!60!black] (0.7,1.5) -- (2.8,1.5) -- (2.8,0.3) -- (0.7,0.3);
  \draw[thick,blue!60!black] (1.5,1.5) -- (1.5,1.15);
  \draw[thick,blue!60!black] (1.3,1.15) rectangle (1.7,0.65);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.5,0.9) {\tiny narrow};
  \draw[-{Latex},blue!60!black] (1.0,1.65) -- (2.2,1.65);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,blue!60!black] at (1.6,1.9) {flow};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (2.5,1.7) {high $P$};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (2.5,0.1) {low $P$};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.4,-0.45) {water circuit};
  % right: electric
  \begin{scope}[xshift=5.2cm]
    \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l=cell] (0,2.0)
          -- (2.6,2.0)
          to[R, l=$R$] (2.6,0)
          -- (0,0);
    \draw[-{Latex}] (0.5,2.2) -- (2.0,2.2);
    \node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.25,2.45) {$I$};
    \node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.3,-0.45) {electric circuit};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.40\textwidth}p{0.52\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Water & Electricity \\
\midrule
Water (amount that flows) & Charge $Q$ \\
Flow rate (volume per second) & Current $I=Q/t$ \\
Pump (raises the water's energy) & Cell / e.m.f.\ $\mathcal{E}$ (raises charge's energy) \\
Water pressure at a point & Electric potential at a point \\
Pressure difference across a section & Potential difference (p.d.) $V$ \\
Narrow pipe / constriction (harder to push through) & Resistance $R$ \\
Wide open pipe (same pressure along it) & Ideal wire (equipotential) \\
Water is not ``used up''; it returns to the pump & Charge is not ``used up''; it returns to the cell \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent\textit{Limits of the analogy.}
Charge is not a fluid you can see, and electrons drift slowly; still, the mapping above is enough for
potential, p.d., e.m.f., and equipotential.

\subsubsection*{Electric potential}

\subsection{Equipotential: same-colour wire method}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.12\textwidth}p{0.82\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Step & \\
\midrule
1 & Paint every continuous ideal wire one colour. Same colour $\Leftrightarrow$ same potential. \\
2 & Colour can change only across a resistor / bulb / meter. \\
3 & An ideal short merges two colours into one. \\
4 & Each colour is one node; then reduce series / parallel. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\ex
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.92\linewidth]{../questions/2017_q24.png}
\end{center}
All resistors are identical. Find the p.d.\ between $X$ and $Y$.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{Method (same-colour wires).}
Paint the paper diagram:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
  \item whole left rail (battery $+$) $=$ one colour, say red;
  \item whole right rail through $Y$ (battery $-$) $=$ blue;
  \item junction $X$ (between resistors) $=$ green.
\end{itemize}
So $Y$ is at $0\,\mathrm{V}$ and $V_{XY}$ is just the potential at $X$.

The top resistor joins red to blue (across the full $9\,\mathrm{V}$).
The bottom path is: red $\xrightarrow{R}$ green $(X)$ $\xrightarrow{R\parallel R}$ blue $(Y)$.
\[
R\parallel R = \frac{R}{2},\qquad
V_{XY}=9\cdot\frac{R/2}{R+R/2}=9\cdot\frac{1}{3}=3.0\,\mathrm{V}.
\]
\sol[2.5cm]{%
$3.0\,\mathrm{V}$ (option B).
}

\ex
\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.1, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l=$12\,\mathrm{V}$] (0,2.6)
        to[R, l=$4\,\Omega$] (2.6,2.6)
        to[R, l=$4\,\Omega$] (5.2,2.6)
        -- (5.2,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw (2.6,2.6) node[above,font=\scriptsize] {$X$}
        to[R, l_=$8\,\Omega$] (2.6,0);
  \draw[thick] (2.6,2.6) -- (3.5,2.6) -- (3.5,0) -- (2.6,0);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (3.9,1.3) {ideal short};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}
An ideal short joins $X$ to the bottom rail. Find the current from the cell.

\medskip
\noindent\textit{Method.}
Bottom rail and short are the same colour $\Rightarrow$ $X$ is forced to that colour.
The $8\,\Omega$ and the right-hand $4\,\Omega$ both sit between two points of the \emph{same}
colour $\Rightarrow$ p.d.\ $=0$ across each $\Rightarrow$ both are bypassed.
Only the left-hand $4\,\Omega$ remains across $12\,\mathrm{V}$:
\[
I=\frac{12}{4}=3.0\,\mathrm{A}.
\]
\sol[2.2cm]{%
$I=3.0\,\mathrm{A}$.
}

\subsection{Potential along a path (``hill diagram'')}

