\section{Current}

Current $I$ is the \textbf{rate of flow of electric charge}: how much charge passes a point per second.
\[
I=\frac{Q}{t},\qquad 1\,\mathrm{A}=1\,\mathrm{C\,s^{-1}}.
\]

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.38\textwidth}p{0.54\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Idea} & \textbf{What it means} \\
\midrule
Conventional current
  & Defined as the direction \emph{positive} charge would flow: $+$ terminal $\to$ external circuit $\to$ $-$ terminal.
    Use this on diagrams unless the question asks about electrons. \\
Electron flow (in metals)
  & The actual mobile charges are free electrons. They drift from $-$ to $+$
    (opposite to conventional $I$), on top of random thermal motion. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{Current and charge conservation}

Charge is conserved. Like water flowing in a closed loop of pipe, charge flowing in a closed circuit
does \textbf{not} accumulate or disappear at any point. The total charge entering a point is always
equal to the total charge leaving the point.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.95]
  % closed water-pipe loop
  \draw[line width=2.2pt, yellow!70!orange]
    (0,0) -- (0,2.6) -- (4.2,2.6) -- (4.2,0) -- (0,0);
  % pump
  \draw[fill=gray!25] (1.5,-0.15) rectangle (2.7,0.45);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (2.1,0.15) {water pump};
  % water wheel (load)
  \draw[fill=gray!20] (0.15,2.15) circle (0.28);
  \foreach \a in {0,60,120} {
    \draw[thick] (0.15,2.15) -- ++(\a:0.28);
  }
  \node[font=\scriptsize,above] at (0.15,2.55) {water wheel};
  % tap
  \draw[thick] (3.7,2.6) -- (3.7,2.95) -- (4.0,2.95);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,above] at (3.85,3.05) {tap};
  % flow arrow
  \draw[-{Latex},thick,orange!80!black] (0.45,0.5) -- (0.45,2.1)
    node[midway,right,font=\scriptsize,orange!80!black] {water current};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (2.1,-0.7) {steady flow in a closed loop (analogy of a circuit)};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

In terms of current, at any point
\[
\boxed{\text{total current in}=\text{total current out}.}
\]

