\title{\textbf{4.2 Electric circuits}}

\ex
A battery is connected across a network of four identical resistors. What are the ammeter
readings $I_1$ to $I_4$?
\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.0, transform shape]
  % battery across the top
  \draw (0,4.2) to[battery1] (7.0,4.2);
  % left: known 2 A, then series R
  \draw (0,4.2) to[ammeter, l=$2\,\mathrm{A}$] (0,2.6)
        to[R] (0,0.8)
        -- (0,0);
  % bottom series: I1 then R, then junction
  \draw (0,0) to[ammeter, l_=$I_1$] (2.4,0)
        to[R] (4.6,0);
  \node[circ] at (4.6,0) {};
  % upper parallel branch: I2 then R
  \draw (4.6,0) -- (4.6,1.6)
        to[ammeter, l=$I_2$] (5.8,1.6)
        to[R] (7.0,1.6)
        -- (7.0,0);
  % lower parallel branch: I3 then R
  \draw (4.6,0) -- (4.6,-1.6)
        to[ammeter, l_=$I_3$] (5.8,-1.6)
        to[R] (7.0,-1.6)
        -- (7.0,0);
  \node[circ] at (7.0,0) {};
  % right return: I4 up to battery
  \draw (7.0,0) to[ammeter, l=$I_4$] (7.0,4.2);
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}
\sol[3.5cm]{%
The two series resistors and the return path carry the full battery current:
$I_1=2\,\mathrm{A}$, $I_4=2\,\mathrm{A}$.
The last two identical resistors are in parallel, so they share equally:
$I_2=I_3=1\,\mathrm{A}$.
}

