\subsubsection*{Water-pipe analogy}

Use the \emph{same} closed water loop as for current:

\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.38\linewidth]{fig_water_loop.png}
\end{center}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.42\textwidth}p{0.50\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Water system & Electric circuit \\
\midrule
Water flowing in the pipe & Charge $Q$ \\
Water current (flow rate) & Electric current $I=Q/t$ \\
Water pump & Cell (source of e.m.f.) \\
\textbf{Water pressure difference}
  (how hard the pump pushes / drop across the wheel or tap)
  & \textbf{Voltage} $=$ potential difference $V$ \\
Water wheel / tap (takes energy from the flow)
  & Load (bulb, resistor,\ldots): converts electrical energy \\
Water is not used up; it returns to the pump
  & Charge is not used up; it returns to the cell \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

So in the water picture, \textbf{voltage $=$ pressure difference}.
A large p.d.\ is like a large pressure drop: each coulomb (each ``bit of water'')
carries / loses more energy between those two points.
The pump sets up the pressure difference (e.m.f.); the wheel and tap are where
pressure drops as energy is delivered.

\noindent\textit{Limits of the analogy.}
Charge is not a fluid you can see, and electrons drift slowly; still, this mapping is enough for
potential, p.d., e.m.f., and equipotential.

