\subsubsection*{Voltage $=$ energy per coulomb}

In the water picture, a bigger pressure boost from the pump means each litre gains more energy.
In a circuit, \textbf{voltage} measures that energy change \emph{per coulomb}:
\[
V=\frac{E}{Q},\qquad 1\,\mathrm{V}=1\,\mathrm{J\,C^{-1}}.
\]

\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.72\linewidth]{fig_voltage_1p5V.png}
\end{center}

A cell labelled $1.5\,\mathrm{V}$ means: as charge is driven through the cell, the flow gains
$1.5\,\mathrm{J}$ for every $1\,\mathrm{C}$ that passes --- the chemical ``pump'' raises the
energy of the charge (this energy boost is the e.m.f.\ of the cell).
Across a bulb or resistor the same idea appears as a \emph{drop}: each coulomb
\emph{loses} energy, converted to light/heat.

\subsubsection*{Electric potential}

Electric potential at a point is the \textbf{electrical energy per coulomb} at that point,
measured relative to an agreed zero (usually the negative terminal of the cell, or earth).

