\subsubsection*{Potential in a circuit}

A p.d.\ is a difference in potential.
Potential at a point is the p.d.\ between that point and an agreed zero
(often an \textbf{earthed} point, taken as $0$).

\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.05, transform shape]
  \draw (0,2.6) to[battery1, l={$\mathcal{E}$}] (4.8,2.6);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,above] at (0.15,2.6) {$+$};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,above] at (4.65,2.6) {$-$};
  \draw (0,2.6) -- (0,0) -- (4.8,0) -- (4.8,2.6);
  \draw (0.6,0) to[bulb, l_={$2\,\mathrm{V}$}] (2.2,0)
        to[R, l_={$4\,\mathrm{V}$}] (4.2,0);
  \node[circ] at (0.6,0) {};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,below left] at (0.55,-0.05) {$A$};
  \node[circ] at (2.2,0) {};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,below] at (2.2,-0.35) {$B$};
  \node[circ] at (4.2,0) {};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,below right] at (4.25,-0.05) {$C$};
  \draw[-{Latex},thick] (0.35,2.2) -- (0.35,0.5);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (0.65,1.3) {$I$};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}

Here the bulb drops $2\,\mathrm{V}$ and the resistor $4\,\mathrm{V}$, so $\mathcal{E}=6\,\mathrm{V}$.
Changing which point is earthed shifts every potential, but all \emph{differences} stay the same:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}cccc@{}}
\toprule
earthed point & potential at $A$ & at $B$ & at $C$ \\
\midrule
$A$ & $0$ & $-2\,\mathrm{V}$ & $-6\,\mathrm{V}$ \\
$B$ & $+2\,\mathrm{V}$ & $0$ & $-4\,\mathrm{V}$ \\
$C$ & $+6\,\mathrm{V}$ & $+4\,\mathrm{V}$ & $0$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

A potential \emph{rise} across the source is the e.m.f.; a potential \emph{drop} across a load is its p.d.
Tracing rises and falls keeps track of energy transfer along the path.
If $C$ is earthed, the potential along the loop looks like this:

