\subsubsection*{Electromotive force (e.m.f.)}

e.m.f.\ $\mathcal{E}$ is the energy \emph{gained} by each coulomb \textbf{inside a source}
(chemical energy $\to$ electrical energy in a cell).
It is \emph{not} the energy spent in a bulb; that is a p.d.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.46\textwidth}p{0.46\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{e.m.f.\ $\mathcal{E}$} & \textbf{p.d.\ $V$} \\
\midrule
Where energy is \emph{gained} (inside the cell)
&
Where energy is \emph{converted / spent} (in a load)
\\[0.35em]
$\mathcal{E}=4\,\mathrm{V}$ $\Leftrightarrow$ each coulomb
gains $4\,\mathrm{J}$ in the battery
&
$V=1\,\mathrm{V}$ across a bulb $\Leftrightarrow$ each coulomb
loses $1\,\mathrm{J}$ in that bulb
\\[0.35em]
Symbol: $\mathcal{E}$ (script E)
&
Symbol: $V$ (also called voltage)
\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.05, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l={$\mathcal{E}=4\,\mathrm{V}$}] (0,2.5)
        to[R, l={$R_1$}, a={$V_1$}] (3.0,2.5)
        to[R, l_={$R_2$}, a^={$V_2$}] (3.0,0)
        -- (0,0);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (-1.15,1.25) {gain};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.5,3.0) {spend};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (4.0,1.25) {spend};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}

\noindent\textit{Example.}
A cell of e.m.f.\ $1.5\,\mathrm{V}$ gives $1.5\,\mathrm{J}$ of electrical energy to every coulomb that
passes through it. If $4\,\mathrm{C}$ pass through the cell, energy supplied $=1.5\times 4=6\,\mathrm{J}$.

\subsubsection*{Equipotential}

Two points are equipotential if they are at the \textbf{same} potential.
Then the p.d.\ between them is zero: no energy is converted when charge moves from one to the other
along an ideal path joining them.

\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.05, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1] (0,2.4)
        -- (4.0,2.4) -- (4.0,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw (1.0,2.4) to[R, l=$R$] (3.0,2.4);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (0.4,2.65) {$A$};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (3.6,2.65) {$C$};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (2.0,0.25) {$B$};
  \draw[thick,red!75!black] (0,2.4) -- (1.0,2.4);
  \draw[thick,red!75!black] (0,2.4) -- (0,1.6);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,red!75!black,align=center] at (-0.7,2.0) {same\\potential};
  \draw[thick,blue!70!black] (3.0,2.4) -- (4.0,2.4) -- (4.0,0) -- (0,0);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,blue!70!black,align=center] at (4.6,1.2) {same\\potential};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}

\noindent\textit{Example.}
Ideal connecting wires have (almost) zero resistance, so every point on one continuous wire is
equipotential. That is why we paint a whole rail one colour: p.d.\ along that wire is $0$.
A short circuit forces two points to become equipotential.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.22\textwidth}p{0.72\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Term & In one line \\
\midrule
Potential & Energy per coulomb \emph{at a point} (needs a zero). \\
p.d. & Energy converted per coulomb \emph{between two points}: $V=E/Q$. \\
e.m.f. & Energy gained per coulomb \emph{inside a source}. \\
Equipotential & Same potential $\Rightarrow$ p.d.\ $=0$ between those points. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\ex
\textbf{Puzzle: potential difference.}
In the circuit below, each bulb dissipates $2\,\mathrm{J}$ per coulomb of charge;
each resistor dissipates $4\,\mathrm{J}$ per coulomb.
What are the p.d.s between (a)~points $A$ and $B$, and (b)~points $C$ and $D$?
\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.05, transform shape]
  \draw (0,3.0) to[battery1, l=$6\,\mathrm{V}$] (5.2,3.0);
  \draw (0,3.0) -- (0,0) -- (5.2,0) -- (5.2,3.0);
  % upper branch: bulb, C, R
  \draw (0,2.2) to[bulb] (2.4,2.2)
        to[R] (5.2,2.2);
  \node[circ] at (0,2.2) {};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,left] at (-0.1,2.2) {$A$};
  \node[circ] at (2.4,2.2) {};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,above] at (2.4,2.45) {$C$};
  % lower branch: R, D, bulb
  \draw (0,0.8) to[R] (2.4,0.8)
        to[bulb] (5.2,0.8);
  \node[circ] at (0,0.8) {};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,left] at (-0.1,0.8) {$B$};
  \node[circ] at (2.4,0.8) {};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,below] at (2.4,0.55) {$D$};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}
\sol[3.2cm]{%
Bulb drop $=2\,\mathrm{V}$, resistor drop $=4\,\mathrm{V}$ (since $V=E/Q$).
(a)~$A$ and $B$ lie on the same ideal wire $\Rightarrow$ equipotential $\Rightarrow$
$V_{AB}=0$.
(b)~From the left rail: upper path drops $2\,\mathrm{V}$ to $C$, lower drops $4\,\mathrm{V}$ to $D$.
So $V_C-V_D=2\,\mathrm{V}$ (or $|V_{CD}|=2\,\mathrm{V}$).
}

