\subsubsection*{Voltage $=$ energy per coulomb}

``Voltage'' always means an \textbf{energy change per coulomb}:
\[
V=\frac{E}{Q},\qquad 1\,\mathrm{V}=1\,\mathrm{J\,C^{-1}}.
\]
So if $Q=2\,\mathrm{C}$ of charge changes its electrical energy by $E=3\,\mathrm{J}$,
the voltage involved is $V=3/2=1.5\,\mathrm{V}$.

\paragraph{Hill picture.}
Think of electrical potential like \emph{height} on a hill
(gravitational PE per kilogram $\leftrightarrow$ electrical PE per coulomb):

\begin{center}
