\subsubsection*{The potential ladder (hill diagram)}

Follow one coulomb around a complete loop:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em,itemsep=0.12em,topsep=0.25em]
  \item The source \emph{lifts} it to a higher potential (energy gain per unit charge $=$ e.m.f.).
  \item Each load it passes \emph{lowers} the potential again (energy loss per unit charge $=$ p.d.).
  \item Charge returns to the potential it started at, so
        \[
        \boxed{\text{e.m.f.\ supplied}=\text{sum of potential drops around the loop}.}
        \]
\end{itemize}

A \textbf{hill diagram} (potential ladder) draws this directly:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em,itemsep=0.12em,topsep=0.25em]
  \item Vertical axis $=$ potential (energy per unit positive charge).
  \item Step \emph{up} $=$ energy added by the source.
  \item Step \emph{down} $=$ energy given up in a component (heat, light, \ldots).
  \item Flat along an ideal wire (same potential; no energy change).
  \item Height is potential, \emph{not} kinetic energy.
\end{itemize}

